summary is: {{ 
Doctor: Hello, Robert. How are you feeling today?

Patient: Not good, doctor. I've been having diarrhoea for the past three days.

Doctor: I see. Can you tell me more about your diarrhoea?

Patient: It's been really bad. I keep going back and forth to the toilet very often.

Doctor: OK. And did it start with vomiting?

Patient: Yes, it did. I vomited a couple of times, but then it went away and it's just diarrhoea now.

Doctor: OK. How many times a day are you going to the toilet?

Patient: Around five to six times a day.

Doctor: OK. And is it watery and loose?

Patient: Yes, it is.

Doctor: OK. Is there any blood in your stools?

Patient: No, I don't think so.

Doctor: OK. Have you noticed any other symptoms, like tummy pain?

Patient: Not really, but I feel very dehydrated and can't drink too much.

Doctor: OK. When you say you can't drink too much, why is that?

Patient: I feel like I don't drink enough and then I go to the loo and I feel dehydrated again.

Doctor: OK. Have you managed to eat anything solid?

Patient: Not much. I only have a little bit of an appetite.

Doctor: OK. Have you had any other symptoms, like fever or temperature?

Patient: I feel a little bit warm, but not really a fever. I feel fine now.

Doctor: OK. Have you had any headaches or muscle pains?

Patient: No, not really.

Doctor: OK. And what were you doing when the symptoms started? Are you aware of any triggers?

Patient: I was at home working on my computer and I felt some nausea and then had to vomit and then had diarrhoea.

Doctor: OK. Did you have any takeaway food or any dodgy restaurant food?

Patient: No, I don't remember.

Doctor: OK. Have you been around anyone unwell with diarrhoea recently?

Patient: No, I don't think so.

Doctor: OK. Are you otherwise fit and well?

Patient: Yes, I am.

Doctor: Do you take any regular medications?

Patient: I suffer from asthma and use inhalers infrequently.

Doctor: OK. Do you have any allergies to medications?

Patient: No, I don't think so.

Doctor: OK. And any family history of any medical problems?

Patient: My father had high blood pressure and died of bowel cancer ten years ago. My mother has type two diabetes.

Doctor: OK. Based on what you've told me, it sounds like you're suffering from gastroenteritis, which is just a tummy bug. It's an infection of your tummy and should last about three to five days and get better. To help yourself get better, you can drink plenty of fluids and take some Dioralyte to replenish minerals and vitamins. You can also take some Paracetamol if you're feeling feverish and weak. I would advise you to continue taking time off work for the next day or two until you get better. If you're still not better next week, come back and see me.

Patient: OK, that sounds good. I'll try to drink plenty of fluid and hope it goes away. If it doesn't, I'll call back. Thank you, doctor.

Doctor: You're welcome. I hope you get better soon.

 }}<|eot_id|>
