Adriana, a 26-year-old woman, came to the doctor's office because of a headache that had been bothering her for three days. The headache was around her right eye and came and went, but was mostly present. She had tried taking Ibuprofen, but it didn't help. She had no history of headaches like this, but her mother and sister both have migraines. She was worried that this could be a migraine. The doctor examined her and asked some questions. The doctor found no abnormalities and concluded that the headache was probably similar to a migraine. The doctor prescribed stronger painkillers, including Naproxen, Paracetamol, and Codeine, and asked Adriana to keep a headache diary to see if there were any triggers that could make the headaches worse. The doctor also asked Adriana to come back in a week to two weeks to discuss prophylactic medications if the headaches continue. }}<|eot_id|>
